  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nThe upper pattern revealed by water vapor imagery is dominated\nby the persistent ridge centered over west Texas and a deep \ncut- off low spinning cyclonically over the Great Lakes. \nBetwixt the two, Iowa and Nebraska are under northwest flow\naloft.\n\nRadar returns this evening have been slowly tapering as they've\ndrifted southeast into a drier and more stable airmass. Temps\nand dewpoints are in the 60s as winds have become light and\nvariable. It's a little muggy but pleasant outside. This would\nbe a good set-up for fog, but for the increasing clouds \nexpected overnight. Any development should be patchy at best. \n\nMore showers and storms are expected to develop soon as the \nnocturnal jet gets act together. The jet will reach farther \nnorth than normal, likely initiating the storms in South Dakota.\nThese storms are progged to push south-southeast overnight, \npassing through the Yankton/Sioux City area around 5am (60% \nPoPs), and through Omaha around 9am (35% PoPs) as odds fade. The\narea should be dry by lunch. \n\nAgain, it'll be a bit cloudier than typical, leaving highs\nranging form the upper 70s to mid-80s. \n\nMonday night should be dry.",
